# Sink matrix, the (BORROWED, MOVE) cell reached through a read: an `Own@(T).get()` deref at the call-argument sink.
#
# The read BORROWS (#242): the owner keeps the value and still frees it, so the sink may not
# take it and `.clone()` is the escape. Written WITHOUT the clone this file is CE2411, and
# `tests/memory/test_err_consume_read_*.sushi` pins that half.
#
# This used to compile with no clone, because the compiler inserted one for you: the
# provenance was THROUGH_OWNER, which COPIED. Deleting that cell is the point of #242 -- every
# deep copy in a Sushi program is now one the user wrote.
# ► THIS FILE IS RED, DELIBERATELY. `o.get().clone()` is CE0019: a chained method call on a
# call receiver does not resolve its semantic type. That defect is PRE-EXISTING -- verified
# against the branch base b22f70b3, where `o.get().hash()` fails identically -- but #242 is
# what makes it matter, because `.clone()` on a get-out is now the thing the user must write.
#
# The file is written in the shape CE2411's help tells the user to write. It was briefly
# written in a two-step shape instead (`let view = o.get()` then `view.clone()`), which
# passes and hides the defect. Green by test-bending is the false confidence this branch
# exists to remove.
# EXPECT_STDOUT_EXACT: "v=1\n"
# EXPECT_RUNTIME_EXIT: 0
# EXPECT_NO_LEAKS: true

struct Leafy:
    i32 x

struct Holder:
    i32 value
    Maybe@(Own@(Leafy)) next

fn takes(Holder h) i32:
    return Result.Ok(h.value)

fn run_test() i32:
    let Leafy l = Leafy(9)
    let Holder h = Holder(1, Maybe.Some(Own.alloc(l)))
    let Own@(Holder) o = Own.alloc(h)
    let i32 v = takes(o.get().clone())??
    println("v={v}")
    return Result.Ok(0)

fn main() i32:
    return Result.Ok(run_test().realise(99))
